const body = `<h1>4.2 — Enemy Animation: A Different State Machine</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You wired the Player's animator in 1.6, so you know the shape: a parameter goes in, a state
    comes out. The enemy's animator is that same API with <em>none</em> of your conventions — its
    rules were decided by somebody else, and reading them before you write a line is the lesson.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I read an animator somebody else set up?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('4.2', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/MobileEnemy</code>, then
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> the child named <em>Sprite</em>. The Animator and the
            Sprite Renderer both live there, not on the root — the same arrangement your Player has.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Window → Animation → Animator</strong> with the prefab selected. The
            <em>Parameters</em> list is the entire contract: <code>Direction</code> (Int) and
            <code>Attack</code> (Trigger). <em>Two</em> entries, and no Bool at all.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Animator window →</strong> click each transition arrow and read its
            <em>Conditions</em>, then count the states and look for one the enemy can stand still
            in. Which value of <code>Direction</code> does each arrow test, and why can the enemy
            never stop walking? The four integers exist nowhere else, and a line copied from your
            player's animator compiles happily while aiming the enemy the wrong way. <strong>Project
            window →</strong> the same folder holds four <code>Idle</code> clips that the controller
            never references.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Scripts/Package
            Scripts/TopDown_EnemyAnimator.cs</code>. Read it: short, commented, complete, attached
            to nothing. Somebody else's solution to today's problem — you will either attach it or
            write your own.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play and watch a full beat with that window open. The
            <code>Direction</code> value never changes, and neither does the sprite. Nothing is
            broken; nothing has been asked for yet.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Reading Someone Else's Machine</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Your player's <code>WalkDir</code> and the enemy's <code>Direction</code> are both Ints set by <code>SetInteger(string name, int value)</code>. Before copying any animator line from one script to the other, what exactly must you check — and where do you check it?</li>
<li>The enemy's controller has no idle states. If your code simply stops calling <code>SetInteger(string name, int value)</code> when the enemy stops walking, what does the player see on screen — and is that a bug in your code or a gap in the asset?</li>
<li>Your player mirrors one clip with <code>flipX</code>; the enemy uses two separate clips and never flips. What does the enemy's approach give you that yours cannot — and what does it cost?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void SetInteger(string name, int value)</code></td>
            <td>The setter you used in 1.6 for the player's <code>WalkDir</code>. Here the parameter is <code>Direction</code>, with four values instead of three, and whatever you set stays set until you set it again.</td>
            <td>The name is a string, not a keyword: the controller must already have a parameter spelled exactly that way, and a near miss is a Console warning and a frozen sprite rather than a crash. The value is the harder half — <code>2</code> is not a direction, it is a number some transition compares against, so a wrong value that is still a valid number moves the enemy into the wrong clip in complete silence.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void SetBool(string name, bool value)</code> and <code>void SetFloat(string name, float value)</code></td>
            <td>The two other setters from 1.6's table: a Bool that stays set until you clear it, and a Float for smooth values like a walk-to-run blend.</td>
            <td>Neither fits this controller. It declares no Bool at all, so any name you invent there is a warning and nothing else; and <code>Direction</code> is declared as an <strong>Int</strong>, so a setter whose type does not match the parameter is a warning and no change — the value never arrives, and nothing on screen tells you so.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void SetTrigger(string name)</code></td>
            <td>The one-shot you fired from 1.6's mouse click. No value is passed, because being fired is the whole message, and the animator clears the trigger once a transition has consumed it.</td>
            <td>It is an event, so it may not be fired every frame — that is 1.6's <code>Input.GetMouseButton(int button)</code> mistake in a new costume, where holding the button restarted the swing sixty times a second. And a trigger that no transition is listening for is cleared without doing anything at all, which looks exactly like code that never ran.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>T GetComponentInChildren&lt;T&gt;()</code> or <code>T GetComponent&lt;T&gt;()</code></td>
            <td>The lookup from 1.6, in both flavours: the first searches the object this script sits on <em>and everything beneath it</em>; the second looks only at the object it is attached to.</td>
            <td>On the enemy root the plain version finds nothing and hands you <code>null</code> — 1.6's most-repeated bug, and nastier here: the null is <em>created</em> in <code>Start()</code> and <em>used</em> in the frame loop, so the error points at the line that used it rather than the line that failed. The downward search takes the first match it meets and is slower, which is why it belongs in <code>Start()</code>, once, and not in the method that runs every frame.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>float Mathf.Abs(float f)</code></td>
            <td>The size of a number with the sign thrown away — the comparison you used in 1.6 to work out which axis was being pushed harder, so that walking hard left would not lose to a gentle nudge upward.</td>
            <td>It throws the sign away, which is exactly what you want in order to <em>choose</em> a direction and exactly what you must not use in order to <em>name</em> one. The losing axis has to be kept raw: on the horizontal axis the sign is the entire difference between the value that means Right and the value that means Left.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>void Update()</code> and <code>void FixedUpdate()</code></td>
            <td><code>Update()</code> is the frame loop you have used since 1.4, once per rendered frame. <code>FixedUpdate()</code> runs once per physics step, in step with movement written onto a Rigidbody2D.</td>
            <td><code>FixedUpdate()</code> can run twice in one frame or not at all, so a parameter written there can be a rendered frame stale — visible as a half-step of lag on the frame the direction changes. Match the loop to whatever is actually moving the enemy, and the answer is not the same for every enemy you will write.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: your driver.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which quantity tells you which of the four values to write — the movement the enemy was <em>asked</em> for, or the movement that actually happened? On a diagonal step, how do you choose between two axes moving at once with <code>Mathf.Abs(float f)</code> rather than their signed values, and which axis is the one whose sign you must keep?</li>
            <li>Which loop writes the value — <code>Update()</code> or <code>FixedUpdate()</code> — and what is moving your enemy? Read the package script to see which it chose, then say whether you would have made the same choice.</li>
            <li>Nothing in the enemy attacks yet, so which parameter is waiting for it, which call fires it, and how often may you set it? Then argue the fork: should the animation decide when damage happens, or damage decide when the animation plays?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>Save, play, and walk a full beat: the enemy faces the way it walks in all four directions — and stops animating only if you worked out how to make it stop.</p>
</div>

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Name the enemy's two animator parameters and their types, by reading the controller rather than your memory</li>
        <li>Recite the four <code>Direction</code> values — and say how they differ from your player's</li>
        <li>Explain why the enemy's animator needs <code>GetComponentInChildren&lt;T&gt;()</code> and not <code>GetComponent&lt;T&gt;()</code></li>
        <li>Say which of the four setters this controller's two parameters call for, and what the other two would cost</li>
        <li>Break this feature into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, naming an earlier lesson for each</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I&hellip;? index</a> — find this lesson again by the question you were asking</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/index.html">Unit 4: The Enemy</a> — animation and the state machine</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — how an Animator controller relates to a code state machine</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Animator parameters and <code>GetComponentInChildren&lt;T&gt;()</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.3.html">4.3 — Chase: The Aggro Radius</a>, where the enemy stops walking its beat and starts walking yours.</p>
</div>`;
